Here’s a simple example of how you can draw a chessboard in LaTeX:

\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tikz}
\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8]
\draw [step=1cm,gray,very thin] (0,0) grid (8,8);
\foreach \x in {1,2,...,8} {
\foreach \y in {1,2,...,8} {
\fill [white] (\x,\y) circle (0.4);
\fill [black] (\x,\y-1) circle (0.4);
}
}
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}


This code uses the tikz package to draw a chessboard. The grid command creates a grid of 8×8 squares with a step size of 1 cm. The foreach loop draws a white square at each point in the grid, while the second foreach loop draws a black square one square below each white square. The circle command draws a circle with a radius of 0.4 cm at each point.

