A sentence in first-order logic is a well-formed formula of the first-order logic. The syntax of a sentence is defined recursively, as follows:

• A sentence is a variable;
• A sentence is the result of applying the connective ∧ (conjunction) to two sentences;
• A sentence is the result of applying the connective ∨ (disjunction) to two sentences;
• A sentence is the result of applying the connective ¬ (negation) to a sentence;
• A sentence is the result of applying the connective → (implication) to two sentences;
• A sentence is the result of applying the quantifier ∃ to a variable and a sentence;
• A sentence is the result of applying the quantifier ∀ to a variable and a sentence;
• A sentence is the result of applying the predicate soldier to a variable;
• A sentence is the result of applying the predicate general to two variables;
• A sentence is the result of applying the predicate assistant to two variables.

The sentence is valid if the following holds:

• A sentence is valid if it is a variable;
• A sentence is valid if the two sentences that it is the result of applying the connective ∧ to are both valid;
• A sentence is valid if the two sentences that it is the result of applying the connective ∨ to are both valid;
• A sentence is valid if the sentence that it is the result of applying the connective ¬ to is not valid;
• A sentence is valid if the two sentences that it is the result of applying the connective → to are not both valid;
• A sentence is valid if the variable and the sentence that it is the result of applying the quantifier ∃ to are valid;
• A sentence is valid if the variable and the sentence that it is the result of applying the quantifier ∀ to are valid;
• A sentence is valid if the variable that it is the result of applying the predicate soldier to is a variable;
• A sentence is valid if the two variables that it is the result of applying the predicate general to are both variables;
• A sentence is valid if the two variables that it is the result of applying the predicate assistant to are both variables.

The given sentence is valid, so its translation into natural language should be "For every soldier, there exists a general who is his assistant.".

